An autumn evening, 1792.

3
00:00:08,611 --> 00:00:10,601
A young teacher named John Dalton is

4
00:00:10,651 --> 00:00:12,791
looking at a flower by candlelight.

5
00:00:13,605 --> 00:00:16,045
It's a geranium, nothing rare.

6
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:18,450
The kind of flower you've probably walked

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:19,780
past this week.

8
00:00:21,156 --> 00:00:23,386
By day, this flower has always looked to

9
00:00:23,436 --> 00:00:25,696
him almost exactly sky-blue.

10
00:00:26,471 --> 00:00:29,331
Tonight, by the candle, it's different.

11
00:00:29,985 --> 00:00:32,395
In his own words, it is astonishingly

12
00:00:32,445 --> 00:00:33,500
changed.

13
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,160
It's now what he calls red.

14
00:00:37,596 --> 00:00:39,626
To the friends he asks, it looks much the

15
00:00:39,676 --> 00:00:41,676
same by day or by candle.

16
00:00:42,331 --> 00:00:44,721
Of all of them, only one sees what he

17
00:00:44,771 --> 00:00:45,800
sees.

18
00:00:46,145 --> 00:00:47,805
His brother, Jonathan.

19
00:00:49,281 --> 00:00:51,461
So who's right about this flower?

20
00:00:52,216 --> 00:00:53,566
By the end of this episode,

21
00:00:53,616 --> 00:00:55,666
you might not be so sure where the pink in

22
00:00:55,716 --> 00:00:57,136
it actually is.

23
00:00:57,350 --> 00:00:59,050
Even on the screen in front of you.

24
00:01:00,455 --> 00:01:05,255
Look closely, and that pink is
tiny red, green and blue lights.

25
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:08,120
Not one of them is pink.

26
00:01:09,092 --> 00:01:10,762
It sounds like a silly question,

27
00:01:10,812 --> 00:01:13,372
but Dalton is not a silly man.

28
00:01:13,967 --> 00:01:16,317
Later, he'll be remembered as a pioneer of

29
